WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:02.810 --> 00:00:04.170
It's like a religious right

00:00:04.170 --> 00:00:06.313
for anyone raised in Texas.

00:00:08.670 --> 00:00:10.950
You roll into the gas station,

00:00:10.950 --> 00:00:12.260
look gorgeous

00:00:13.600 --> 00:00:16.300 position:63%
and pump your large vehicle to the brim.

00:00:16.300 --> 00:00:19.740
With that sumptuous, glistening gas

00:00:19.740 --> 00:00:23.143 position:66%
that turn the state into an energy empire.

00:00:28.660 --> 00:00:29.753
Smell that?

00:00:32.360 --> 00:00:35.520 position:61%
It's a rush of petrochemical pheromones

00:00:35.520 --> 00:00:37.183
straight to the brain.

00:00:38.270 --> 00:00:42.440
The blessing of oil has given
Texas many wonderful things.

00:00:42.440 --> 00:00:45.630 position:66%
The great fracking prairies of west Texas,

00:00:45.630 --> 00:00:47.803
JR Ewing, Enron,

00:00:48.790 --> 00:00:52.200
and of course the petro
paradise of Pasadena

00:00:52.200 --> 00:00:55.050 position:66%
with its chemical factories that celebrate

00:00:55.050 --> 00:00:59.403
humanity's ability to harness
and then dominate nature.

00:01:00.360 --> 00:01:01.880
Imagine my surprise then

00:01:01.880 --> 00:01:04.600 position:56%
to learn that right here in Houston,

00:01:04.600 --> 00:01:07.430
there were people like this,

00:01:07.430 --> 00:01:11.370
a pair of anti-petro hippies.

00:01:11.370 --> 00:01:14.500
These two guys say they can
make things like fertilizers,

00:01:14.500 --> 00:01:17.060
and cleaning products, and plastics

00:01:17.060 --> 00:01:20.460 position:59%
without any oil or natural gas at all.

00:01:20.460 --> 00:01:23.430
And they say they can do
it at this factory which,

00:01:23.430 --> 00:01:24.960
if you believe them,

00:01:24.960 --> 00:01:27.973 position:56%
does not produce one ounce of waste.

00:01:29.250 --> 00:01:30.850
In honor of Jerry Jones

00:01:30.850 --> 00:01:33.580
and all that Texas considers Holy

00:01:33.580 --> 00:01:38.150
I decided to investigate this
place and these bold claims.

00:01:38.150 --> 00:01:40.953
I busted right on in to Solugen.

00:01:49.510 --> 00:01:51.133
Because Houston is awesome,

00:01:52.690 --> 00:01:55.083 position:64%
it doesn't really believe in zoning laws.

00:01:56.400 --> 00:01:59.270
You can drive through a
neighborhood like this

00:01:59.270 --> 00:02:02.570
and then run smack dab
into a chemical plant.

00:02:02.570 --> 00:02:03.660
In this case,

00:02:03.660 --> 00:02:06.200
the headquarters of Solugen.

00:02:06.200 --> 00:02:10.550
The company has raised more
than $420 million so far

00:02:10.550 --> 00:02:12.510
and counts noted science expert,

00:02:12.510 --> 00:02:15.750
Leonardo DiCaprio, as
one of its investors.

00:02:15.750 --> 00:02:18.450
Tell me the big idea
nobody's ever heard of

00:02:18.450 --> 00:02:20.490
synthetic biology or any
of this stuff before,

00:02:20.490 --> 00:02:23.390
what's the very core thing
you guys are going after?

00:02:23.390 --> 00:02:25.520 position:58%
Yeah, I think like there's a few ways

00:02:25.520 --> 00:02:26.810
to think about what we do.

00:02:26.810 --> 00:02:28.540
At the simplest level,

00:02:28.540 --> 00:02:29.850
the question that we want to answer

00:02:29.850 --> 00:02:32.430
is how do we use carbon dioxide

00:02:32.430 --> 00:02:34.810 position:63%
to make valuable chemicals and materials

00:02:34.810 --> 00:02:36.410
that we interact with every day?

00:02:37.760 --> 00:02:39.300
The star of the show here

00:02:39.300 --> 00:02:43.150
is something that
Solugen calls a Bioforge.

00:02:43.150 --> 00:02:46.220
The big idea behind all
this machinery is this:

00:02:46.220 --> 00:02:48.850 position:58%
you feed some enzymes with corn syrup

00:02:48.850 --> 00:02:50.730 position:56%
and because they're special enzymes,

00:02:50.730 --> 00:02:53.626
they kick off useful
molecules that can be refined

00:02:53.626 --> 00:02:56.460 position:58%
and then used for all sorts of tasks.

00:02:56.460 --> 00:02:58.320
In short, you're making chemicals

00:02:58.320 --> 00:03:02.520
but using corn instead of
oil or gas to produce them.

00:03:02.520 --> 00:03:03.760
As Solugen sees it,

00:03:03.760 --> 00:03:06.180 position:64%
it's technology strips a lot of steps out

00:03:06.180 --> 00:03:08.330
of the chemical making process.

00:03:08.330 --> 00:03:10.100 position:59%
And most importantly for mother earth,

00:03:10.100 --> 00:03:12.630
eliminates all the noxious waste

00:03:12.630 --> 00:03:14.680 position:59%
of traditional chemical manufacturing.

00:03:14.680 --> 00:03:16.630
I actually grew up in Houston.

00:03:16.630 --> 00:03:17.463
I, you know,

00:03:17.463 --> 00:03:19.130
Pasadena is not that far away

00:03:19.130 --> 00:03:23.260
and it's famous as like
this chemical industry town

00:03:23.260 --> 00:03:25.640
sort of notorious for
the smells and things

00:03:25.640 --> 00:03:27.660
that come out of there.

00:03:27.660 --> 00:03:28.493
So, you know,

00:03:28.493 --> 00:03:31.580
if I think about the
traditional chemical process,

00:03:31.580 --> 00:03:35.570
what goes into that and why
is it notorious in some ways,

00:03:35.570 --> 00:03:37.060
for all the waste products?

00:03:37.060 --> 00:03:39.080 position:56%
If you think about a chemical plant,

00:03:39.080 --> 00:03:41.620
like the plants in Pasadena
that you grew up with,

00:03:41.620 --> 00:03:43.080
all they're really trying to do

00:03:43.080 --> 00:03:45.500
is convert a feedstock
into a valuable product.

00:03:45.500 --> 00:03:46.680 position:61%
And when you guys talk about feedstock,

00:03:46.680 --> 00:03:49.110
that's just the base thing
that you're starting with-

00:03:49.110 --> 00:03:50.205
The raw material.
The raw materials.

00:03:50.205 --> 00:03:51.140
And the problem with

00:03:51.140 --> 00:03:53.605 position:58%
the traditional chemicals industry is

00:03:53.605 --> 00:03:56.930
you can't convert your feedstock
directly to the product.

00:03:56.930 --> 00:03:59.210
What happens is you
make this alphabet soup

00:03:59.210 --> 00:04:00.400
of other side products,

00:04:00.400 --> 00:04:01.858 position:59%
these toxic emissions, all the smells,

00:04:01.858 --> 00:04:03.460
all these waste streams.

00:04:03.460 --> 00:04:05.951 position:64%
So really what the Solugen innovation is,

00:04:05.951 --> 00:04:07.470
Gorab and I coming together,

00:04:07.470 --> 00:04:08.900
is about, okay, well,

00:04:08.900 --> 00:04:12.948
what if you could convert a
feedstock directly to a product?

00:04:12.948 --> 00:04:14.170
What Solugen is claiming

00:04:14.170 --> 00:04:16.630
is that it has developed
a completely new way

00:04:16.630 --> 00:04:17.850
of making chemicals.

00:04:17.850 --> 00:04:21.000
That's both more efficient
than conventional methods

00:04:21.000 --> 00:04:23.960 position:64%
and, almost disturbingly, pollution free.

00:04:23.960 --> 00:04:28.960 position:56%
Okay, so, sugar, water, carbon go in

00:04:29.290 --> 00:04:32.100
and then what comes out at the end?

00:04:32.100 --> 00:04:33.280
Our products, yeah.

00:04:33.280 --> 00:04:34.230
The product, and no,

00:04:34.230 --> 00:04:37.240
and is there water waste,
but it's kind of cleaner?

00:04:37.240 --> 00:04:38.990
Zero
It's just the product?

00:04:38.990 --> 00:04:39.903
Okay, so, there's like nothing?

00:04:39.903 --> 00:04:41.750
That's the beauty of like,

00:04:41.750 --> 00:04:43.670 position:59%
the insight that we had was just like,

00:04:43.670 --> 00:04:45.610 position:61%
you'll have no air emissions, no water.

00:04:45.610 --> 00:04:47.140
It's a huge advance.

00:04:47.140 --> 00:04:49.110
Because of like CRISPR and-
Precisely.

00:04:49.110 --> 00:04:51.574
And just being able to
change the DNA of it?

00:04:51.574 --> 00:04:52.451
Exactly.

00:04:52.451 --> 00:04:54.690
Change the DNA and then
fundamentally the enzyme

00:04:54.690 --> 00:04:56.690 position:63%
in a way that lets it do things we want.

00:05:00.380 --> 00:05:03.130 position:58%
My tour of Solugen began in its labs.

00:05:03.130 --> 00:05:06.450
The scientists here must
discover new enzymes

00:05:06.450 --> 00:05:08.570
that will perform certain tasks

00:05:08.570 --> 00:05:11.740
and then figure out how
to grow and feed them.

00:05:11.740 --> 00:05:14.110
This is where we actually
engineer those enzymes.

00:05:14.110 --> 00:05:15.840
There's the physical wet lab space,

00:05:15.840 --> 00:05:18.010 position:59%
but then we've got a server in Germany

00:05:18.010 --> 00:05:20.130 position:63%
that is running millions of computations

00:05:20.130 --> 00:05:22.170
on this little enzyme to figure out

00:05:22.170 --> 00:05:24.550
how do we keep optimizing
it to make it better

00:05:24.550 --> 00:05:26.270
for that reactor?

00:05:26.270 --> 00:05:28.560
Once we designed that enzyme,

00:05:28.560 --> 00:05:31.790
we now have to express
it at high enough titers

00:05:31.790 --> 00:05:34.330
that we can use it in
our industrial processes.

00:05:34.330 --> 00:05:36.240
This is a small version

00:05:36.240 --> 00:05:38.710 position:61%
where we actually ferment the proteins.

00:05:38.710 --> 00:05:39.920
And the way we do that here

00:05:39.920 --> 00:05:42.810 position:59%
is we've let it live in this fermentor

00:05:42.810 --> 00:05:45.390
and we feed it, its
nutrients, let it grow,

00:05:45.390 --> 00:05:48.500
and, as you can see, this
started like pretty clear,

00:05:48.500 --> 00:05:49.970
but see how it's dark now?

00:05:49.970 --> 00:05:52.160
That means that protein
is starting to express

00:05:52.160 --> 00:05:53.193
really, really well.

00:05:54.190 --> 00:05:55.260
Everything else that you see here

00:05:55.260 --> 00:05:58.540
is downstream processing to
separate that protein out

00:05:58.540 --> 00:06:02.243
and isolate it so we can
put it into the reactor.

00:06:03.190 --> 00:06:05.098
It all sounds easy enough now,

00:06:05.098 --> 00:06:09.550
but Gorab and Sean have been
on a long and winding journey

00:06:09.550 --> 00:06:11.460
to figure this stuff out.

00:06:11.460 --> 00:06:15.290
How did you two meet and
come up with this idea

00:06:15.290 --> 00:06:16.269
in the first place?

00:06:16.269 --> 00:06:19.650
People think we made the story up,

00:06:19.650 --> 00:06:20.640
but we didn't, right?

00:06:20.640 --> 00:06:22.670 position:66%
So like I'm actually a physician scientist

00:06:22.670 --> 00:06:25.160
studying cancer biology,
discovered really cool,

00:06:25.160 --> 00:06:27.960
like, reactions that
make molecules in cancer.

00:06:27.960 --> 00:06:29.850
Started with hydrogen peroxide.

00:06:29.850 --> 00:06:32.620
And Sean was at MIT doing
his chemical engineering PhD

00:06:32.620 --> 00:06:35.030 position:59%
on the production of hydrogen peroxide

00:06:35.030 --> 00:06:37.600
at massive scales using
metal catalyst, right?

00:06:37.600 --> 00:06:39.230 position:58%
So we're working on the same problem,

00:06:39.230 --> 00:06:40.063
but just from-

00:06:40.063 --> 00:06:41.210
Enzymes metals, right?

00:06:41.210 --> 00:06:42.280
It's always just a story of luck.

00:06:42.280 --> 00:06:43.400
Like lucky that we met,

00:06:43.400 --> 00:06:44.260
lucky we had this idea.

00:06:44.260 --> 00:06:45.940
Then what was really lucky is

00:06:45.940 --> 00:06:48.600 position:61%
our first customer saw our pitch to MIT

00:06:48.600 --> 00:06:51.050
and they said, "Hey, if you
make this, we'll buy it."

00:06:51.050 --> 00:06:53.440
And so actually like we
loaded our first product

00:06:53.440 --> 00:06:55.050
in the back of my Subaru

00:06:55.050 --> 00:06:57.110 position:66%
and we drove it to our customer in Dallas.

00:06:57.110 --> 00:06:58.380
Like a tank of something?

00:06:58.380 --> 00:06:59.880
It was just a one gallon bottle.

00:06:59.880 --> 00:07:01.363
It was a mixture of
gluconic acid and peroxide

00:07:01.363 --> 00:07:02.830
that we called Bioperoxide.

00:07:02.830 --> 00:07:04.300
So it'd be used for?

00:07:04.300 --> 00:07:06.110
For a cleaning hot tubs.

00:07:06.110 --> 00:07:07.015
Yeah, so we just-

00:07:07.015 --> 00:07:08.650
It's not glamorous, actually.

00:07:08.650 --> 00:07:09.860
No, there's nothing

00:07:09.860 --> 00:07:11.170
glamorous about this.
But the best part

00:07:11.170 --> 00:07:12.950
is like, we thought it was amazing.

00:07:12.950 --> 00:07:15.090
So like we made this first sale

00:07:15.090 --> 00:07:15.923
and then I told my wife

00:07:15.923 --> 00:07:16.870
I was like, I'm gonna quit my job.

00:07:16.870 --> 00:07:18.588
She was like, you made like what?

00:07:18.588 --> 00:07:19.466 position:58%
She was like "how much was the sale?"

00:07:19.466 --> 00:07:20.383
Like, $200?

00:07:22.135 --> 00:07:22.968
And this machine,

00:07:22.968 --> 00:07:24.330
this is like the first thing that

00:07:24.330 --> 00:07:25.950
you guys worked on?
Where it all started.

00:07:25.950 --> 00:07:26.783
Yeah, yeah.

00:07:26.783 --> 00:07:28.980 position:59%
So this is the original one we've got,

00:07:28.980 --> 00:07:30.580
I don't know why that's there,

00:07:30.580 --> 00:07:32.450 position:66%
but yeah, multiple times people have tried

00:07:32.450 --> 00:07:33.785
to throw this away,

00:07:33.785 --> 00:07:35.554
but yeah, no, I,

00:07:35.554 --> 00:07:39.070 position:59%
Gorab and I, we hand built the system.

00:07:39.070 --> 00:07:40.600
With this particular machine

00:07:40.600 --> 00:07:42.560 position:66%
when you were trying to develop the enzyme

00:07:42.560 --> 00:07:44.930 position:63%
and your technique, there was just like,

00:07:44.930 --> 00:07:46.800 position:58%
nothing like this that you could buy?

00:07:46.800 --> 00:07:49.120
So there are no enzymatic reactors.

00:07:49.120 --> 00:07:50.520
So yeah, you can't go
into Google and be like,

00:07:50.520 --> 00:07:52.370
I want to buy an enzymatic reactor.

00:07:52.370 --> 00:07:53.320
And where did you build this?

00:07:53.320 --> 00:07:55.097
In somebody's apartment, or?

00:07:55.097 --> 00:07:57.300
We rented some lab space

00:07:57.300 --> 00:07:59.090 position:56%
behind Love Field Airport in Dallas.

00:07:59.090 --> 00:08:00.440
We rented some lab space.

00:08:00.440 --> 00:08:02.160
And then this was built
from Home Depot parts.

00:08:02.160 --> 00:08:04.480
We actually had to take out
ceiling panels in the lab

00:08:04.480 --> 00:08:05.313
to fit it in.

00:08:05.313 --> 00:08:06.870 position:56%
How long did it take you to do that?

00:08:06.870 --> 00:08:07.920
This was,

00:08:07.920 --> 00:08:11.880
I would say this was probably
a two or three week build.

00:08:11.880 --> 00:08:12.980
And now these guys'

00:08:12.980 --> 00:08:17.831
bizarre machines are all grown
up and rather spectacular.

00:08:17.831 --> 00:08:22.130
And so this is the first
commercial Bioforge.

00:08:22.130 --> 00:08:24.070 position:56%
All the extra piping and everything,

00:08:24.070 --> 00:08:27.520
I mean, what's going on all
around this on the sides?

00:08:27.520 --> 00:08:29.340
From any point in the plant,

00:08:29.340 --> 00:08:31.640
you can essentially send
material anywhere else

00:08:31.640 --> 00:08:32.580
in the plant.

00:08:32.580 --> 00:08:35.030
This is how you build a
zero discharge facility

00:08:35.030 --> 00:08:36.223
with no waste stream.

00:08:37.490 --> 00:08:38.920
This is corn syrup.

00:08:38.920 --> 00:08:40.810 position:58%
I kind of jokingly say that this tank

00:08:40.810 --> 00:08:42.796
contains 10 million calories.

00:08:42.796 --> 00:08:43.629
10 million calories?

00:08:43.629 --> 00:08:45.570
That's 10 million calories, yeah.

00:08:45.570 --> 00:08:48.150
So once it works its way
through the entire plant,

00:08:48.150 --> 00:08:51.133
then our end product is
stored in these tanks.

00:08:51.133 --> 00:08:54.720
And so we can essentially
take our four ingredients,

00:08:54.720 --> 00:08:56.760
blend them with the
material in these blue tanks

00:08:56.760 --> 00:08:59.560
and then ship finished goods
into the concrete industry,

00:08:59.560 --> 00:09:01.403
into ag, and into water treatment.

00:09:06.410 --> 00:09:08.490 position:64%
How'd you know that's supposed to go off?

00:09:08.490 --> 00:09:09.494
I built this.

00:09:10.960 --> 00:09:13.330
Much of what happens
in this jungle of pipes

00:09:13.330 --> 00:09:16.210 position:56%
relies on Solugen's new-age enzymes.

00:09:16.210 --> 00:09:20.030
But some of the action requires
old fashioned chemistry

00:09:20.030 --> 00:09:22.460
in the form of metal catalysts.

00:09:22.460 --> 00:09:24.940 position:56%
It's the metals and enzymes combined

00:09:24.940 --> 00:09:27.833
that let the company make
just about any chemical

00:09:27.833 --> 00:09:29.720
known to man.

00:09:29.720 --> 00:09:32.950
Plastics, gasoline,
whatever you want to make,

00:09:32.950 --> 00:09:35.670
it depends on what type
of catalyst they touch.

00:09:35.670 --> 00:09:38.669
Here you can make a completely
different product profile

00:09:38.669 --> 00:09:40.810
as this catalyst.

00:09:40.810 --> 00:09:43.397
Like if you come to me
as a customer and say,

00:09:43.397 --> 00:09:45.698
"Hey, I want to make nylon."

00:09:45.698 --> 00:09:49.600
There is a catalyst in there
that when paired with an enzyme

00:09:49.600 --> 00:09:53.317
can make nylon, the
starting precursor to nylon.

00:09:53.317 --> 00:09:55.440
So that's the beauty of
kind of what we're doing

00:09:55.440 --> 00:09:57.950
is like we've got this
sweet hundreds of enzymes,

00:09:57.950 --> 00:09:59.540
hundreds of metal catalysts.

00:09:59.540 --> 00:10:00.970
Now it's just mixing and matching

00:10:00.970 --> 00:10:02.560
based on what customers need

00:10:02.560 --> 00:10:04.310
to make the product that they want.

00:10:07.370 --> 00:10:10.280
One of the biggest promises
behind this technology

00:10:10.280 --> 00:10:13.890
would come from sucking carbon
right out of the atmosphere

00:10:13.890 --> 00:10:16.820
and injecting it into
the Bioforge as feed.

00:10:16.820 --> 00:10:18.260
Instead of the corn syrup

00:10:18.260 --> 00:10:19.890
that they're currently using.

00:10:19.890 --> 00:10:21.857 position:59%
Solugen thinks it knows how to do this

00:10:21.857 --> 00:10:25.200
and is in the midst of
perfecting its techniques.

00:10:25.200 --> 00:10:27.030
Basically that plant out there,

00:10:27.030 --> 00:10:29.450
it makes about 10,000
metric tons of product,

00:10:29.450 --> 00:10:31.600
but it removes about 30,

00:10:31.600 --> 00:10:34.811
greater than 30,000 metric
tons of carbon dioxide,

00:10:34.811 --> 00:10:39.370
which is pretty remarkable
on a mass balance basis.

00:10:39.370 --> 00:10:42.270
How can we use synthetic
biology and marry it

00:10:42.270 --> 00:10:45.250
with traditional
petrochemistry techniques?

00:10:45.250 --> 00:10:46.986 position:56%
Because we need both of those things

00:10:46.986 --> 00:10:50.760
to really capture the essence
of using CO2 as a feedstock.

00:10:50.760 --> 00:10:52.630 position:63%
We were working with the corn story now,

00:10:52.630 --> 00:10:54.460
which is a great CO2 sink,

00:10:54.460 --> 00:10:56.480 position:64%
but the reality is where we want to go is

00:10:56.480 --> 00:10:59.100 position:64%
how do we just take that CO2 from the air

00:10:59.100 --> 00:11:01.510
and make the things that we want?

00:11:01.510 --> 00:11:04.450
The Biforge only opened
a couple of months ago

00:11:04.450 --> 00:11:05.940
and some of the claims around it

00:11:05.940 --> 00:11:08.240
seem somewhere between bold

00:11:08.240 --> 00:11:10.503 position:56%
and the stuff of green tech fantasy.

00:11:11.490 --> 00:11:14.260
Solugen, though, is actually
making things already

00:11:14.260 --> 00:11:16.270 position:56%
and does have some paying customers.

00:11:16.270 --> 00:11:19.460 position:61%
Our chemicals are used in like concrete

00:11:19.460 --> 00:11:22.050 position:66%
and building material and like wood glues.

00:11:22.050 --> 00:11:25.150
So all the boring things that
